{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"id\":\"R-6:88\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The adjacent account calls the straight path Allah’s guidance and says He guides whom He wills; the petition in 1:6 therefore places the speakers’ direction in divine agency. The warning about shirk keeps this gift from implying immunity from failure.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"},{\"ref\":\"6:87\",\"ar\":\"وَٱجْتَبَيْنَٰهُمْ وَهَدَيْنَٰهُمْ إِلَىٰ صِرَٰطٍۢ مُّسْتَقِيمٍۢ\"},{\"ref\":\"6:88\",\"ar\":\"ذَٰلِكَ هُدَى ٱللَّهِ يَهْدِى بِهِۦ مَن يَشَآءُ مِنْ عِبَادِهِۦ ۚ وَلَوْ أَشْرَكُوا۟ لَحَبِطَ عَنْهُم مَّا كَانُوا۟ يَعْمَلُونَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus asks to be guided; the nearby verses describe people whom God selected and led to a straight path.\",\"limits\":\"These verses do not say that receiving guidance makes later choices irrelevant; 6:88 explicitly warns of the consequence of associating partners with God.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-6:90\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"After naming prophets whom Allah guided, 6:90 commands the audience to follow their guidance. The requested path can thus be heard as a way made recognizable through prophetic exemplars.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"},{\"ref\":\"6:90\",\"ar\":\"أُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ ٱلَّذِينَ هَدَى ٱللَّهُ ۖ فَبِهُدَىٰهُمُ ٱقْتَدِهْ\"}],\"activation\":\"Both passages join divine guidance to a way that people can follow; 6:90 supplies named human examples for the plural request.\",\"limits\":\"The excerpt does not identify the focus’s path exclusively with those prophets’ biographies or specify which of their acts is meant to be followed.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-6:126\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The passage first describes divine guidance as opening the chest to Islam, with misguidance as constriction, then names the straight path as the Lord’s. The path requested in 1:6 is therefore set beside an inward opening that makes Islam possible.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"},{\"ref\":\"6:125\",\"ar\":\"فَمَن يُرِدِ ٱللَّهُ أَن يَهْدِيَهُۥ يَشْرَحْ صَدْرَهُۥ لِلْإِسْلَٰمِ ۖ وَمَن يُرِدْ أَن يُضِلَّهُۥ يَجْعَلْ صَدْرَهُۥ ضَيِّقًا حَرَجًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"6:126\",\"ar\":\"وَهَٰذَا صِرَٰطُ رَبِّكَ مُسْتَقِيمًۭا\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus’s hidaya and صراط مستقيم appear together in the neighboring passage as guidance, inward capacity, and a path attributed to the Lord.\",\"limits\":\"The passage does not state that chest-opening exhausts the meaning of guidance or that the path is only an inner state.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-6:127\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Immediately after identifying the Lord’s straight path, the passage names its beneficiaries’ abode as دار السلام and describes their Lord as their guardian. This makes the requested route audible alongside a settled destination and relation to God.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"},{\"ref\":\"6:126\",\"ar\":\"وَهَٰذَا صِرَٰطُ رَبِّكَ مُسْتَقِيمًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"6:127\",\"ar\":\"لَهُمْ دَارُ ٱلسَّلَٰمِ عِندَ رَبِّهِمْ ۖ وَهُوَ وَلِيُّهُم بِمَا كَانُوا۟ يَعْمَلُونَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The consecutive path and abode statements provide a route-and-destination sequence beside the focus’s plea for direction.\",\"limits\":\"The wording does not explicitly say that دار السلام is the endpoint of every use of صراط مستقيم.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-6:153\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The path is singular and commanded to be followed, while plural paths are rejected because they divide people away from God’s way. This gives the focus’s definite straight path a contrast with fragmentation into competing routes.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"},{\"ref\":\"6:153\",\"ar\":\"وَأَنَّ هَٰذَا صِرَٰطِى مُسْتَقِيمًۭا فَٱتَّبِعُوهُ ۖ وَلَا تَتَّبِعُوا۟ ٱلسُّبُلَ فَتَفَرَّقَ بِكُمْ عَن سَبِيلِهِۦ\"}],\"activation\":\"The verse repeats the focus’s صراط and مستقيم together, then explicitly contrasts that way with the plural السبل.\",\"limits\":\"The passage gives no count of historical communities or groups represented by the plural paths.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-6:154\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"After 6:153 commands following the straight path, the next verse recalls Moses receiving a Book described as guidance and mercy. This sequence may present revealed scripture as a historical means of the path requested in 1:6.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"},{\"ref\":\"6:153\",\"ar\":\"وَأَنَّ هَٰذَا صِرَٰطِى مُسْتَقِيمًۭا فَٱتَّبِعُوهُ\"},{\"ref\":\"6:154\",\"ar\":\"وَهُدًۭى وَرَحْمَةًۭ لَّعَلَّهُم بِلِقَآءِ رَبِّهِمْ يُؤْمِنُونَ\"}],\"missing\":\"The sequence does not explicitly identify Moses’s Book with the path of 6:153. A clearer equation between scripture and that path elsewhere in the passage or surah could establish the link.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-6:161\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The straight path is specified as a sound religion, Abraham’s way, and a hanif orientation apart from shirk. The phrase therefore opens into a concrete religious identity rather than remaining an unnamed route.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"},{\"ref\":\"6:161\",\"ar\":\"هَدَىٰنِى رَبِّىٓ إِلَىٰ صِرَٰطٍۢ مُّسْتَقِيمٍۢ دِينًۭا قِيَمًۭا مِّلَّةَ إِبْرَٰهِيمَ حَنِيفًۭا ۚ وَمَا كَانَ مِنَ ٱلْمُشْرِكِينَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The same صراط مستقيم formula is followed by appositional descriptions that name its religious character.\",\"limits\":\"This statement is voiced as a particular proclamation and does not list every dimension of the focus’s path.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-7:16\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Iblis vows to sit for people on God’s straight path. The requested route is thus also a contested passage where opposition seeks to intercept travelers.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"},{\"ref\":\"7:16\",\"ar\":\"قَالَ فَبِمَآ أَغْوَيْتَنِى لَأَقْعُدَنَّ لَهُمْ صِرَٰطَكَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The exact phrase الصراط المستقيم recurs, now possessively attributed to God and made the location of Iblis’s threat.\",\"limits\":\"The verse does not describe how this obstruction works in each person’s life or imply that the path itself is crooked.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-7:43\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"In the Garden, its people say they would not have been guided had Allah not guided them. Their retrospective gratitude sounds like an answer to the focus’s prospective plea.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"},{\"ref\":\"7:43\",\"ar\":\"وَقَالُوا۟ ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ ٱلَّذِى هَدَىٰنَا لِهَٰذَا وَمَا كُنَّا لِنَهْتَدِىَ لَوْلَآ أَنْ هَدَىٰنَا ٱللَّهُ\"}],\"activation\":\"The shared root ه د ي connects a present plural request with a later plural acknowledgment of divine guidance.\",\"limits\":\"The scene does not explicitly identify the Garden as the only intended result of the requested guidance.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-7:146\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Arrogant people see the path of right guidance but refuse to take it, while taking the path of error. The focus’s plea can consequently be heard as asking for a will that accepts guidance, not just access to information about it.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"},{\"ref\":\"7:146\",\"ar\":\"وَإِن يَرَوْا۟ سَبِيلَ ٱلرُّشْدِ لَا يَتَّخِذُوهُ سَبِيلًۭا وَإِن يَرَوْا۟ سَبِيلَ ٱلْغَىِّ يَتَّخِذُوهُ سَبِيلًۭا\"}],\"activation\":\"The two paths are distinguished by the verbs of taking and refusing, and the focus requests to be guided onto its path.\",\"limits\":\"The focus does not itself mention arrogance, nor does this verse use صراط or مستقيم.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-7:178\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The verse pairs Allah’s guiding with a person becoming المهتدي, then pairs misguidance with loss. It frames the focus’s request as dependence on the one who determines whether someone is rightly guided.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"},{\"ref\":\"7:178\",\"ar\":\"مَن يَهْدِ ٱللَّهُ فَهُوَ ٱلْمُهْتَدِى ۖ وَمَن يُضْلِلْ فَأُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ هُمُ ٱلْخَٰسِرُونَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus asks for hidaya; the paired clauses make Allah the subject of both guidance and misguidance.\",\"limits\":\"The verse does not resolve how divine guidance relates to human choice or specify the stages of being guided.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-8:3\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"The root of ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ is shared with يُقِيمُونَ in a verse naming prayer and giving as believer practices. This may connect the requested upright way with enacted worship and generosity.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"},{\"ref\":\"8:3\",\"ar\":\"ٱلَّذِينَ يُقِيمُونَ ٱلصَّلَوٰةَ وَمِمَّا رَزَقْنَٰهُمْ يُنفِقُونَ\"}],\"missing\":\"The shared root alone does not establish that this straight path means these practices. A Quranic passage that joins istiqama or the straight path directly to establishing prayer and giving could confirm the proposed enactment.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-10:9\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Here the Lord guides believers because of their faith, and the scene moves to rivers in Gardens. This links requested guidance with faith and a realized destination, while placing the focus’s imperative beside a later divine action.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"},{\"ref\":\"10:9\",\"ar\":\"يَهْدِيهِمْ رَبُّهُم بِإِيمَٰنِهِمْ\"},{\"ref\":\"10:9\",\"ar\":\"تَجْرِى مِن تَحْتِهِمُ ٱلْأَنْهَٰرُ فِى جَنَّٰتِ ٱلنَّعِيمِ\"}],\"activation\":\"Both passages use guidance language; 10:9 supplies faith and the Garden as elements of the guided believers’ scene.\",\"limits\":\"This verse does not use الصراط المستقيم or state that faith alone exhausts the conditions of guidance.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-10:25\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Allah calls to دار السلام and guides whom He wills to a straight path. The focus’s plea can be heard within this double movement: an invitation toward peace and divine direction along the way.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"},{\"ref\":\"10:25\",\"ar\":\"وَٱللَّهُ يَدْعُوٓا۟ إِلَىٰ دَارِ ٱلسَّلَٰمِ وَيَهْدِى مَن يَشَآءُ إِلَىٰ صِرَٰطٍۢ مُّسْتَقِيمٍۢ\"}],\"activation\":\"The same guidance-to-straight-path construction appears, preceded by a call to the abode of peace.\",\"limits\":\"The indefinite صراط in 10:25 is not grammatically identical in definiteness to the focus’s ٱلصِّرَٰطَ.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-10:35\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The passage asks which guide deserves to be followed and answers that Allah guides to truth. Beside the focus’s plea, guidance becomes a test of the rightful object of following; the previous Fatiha verse has already addressed God as the one worshipped and sought for help.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:5\",\"ar\":\"إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ وَإِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"},{\"ref\":\"10:35\",\"ar\":\"قُلْ هَلْ مِن شُرَكَآئِكُم مَّن يَهْدِىٓ إِلَى ٱلْحَقِّ ۚ قُلِ ٱللَّهُ يَهْدِى لِلْحَقِّ ۗ أَفَمَن يَهْدِىٓ إِلَى ٱلْحَقِّ أَحَقُّ أَن يُتَّبَعَ أَمَّن لَّا يَهِدِّىٓ إِلَّآ أَن يُهْدَىٰ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus follows exclusive worship and reliance with a direct request for guidance; 10:35 makes following the one who truly guides an explicit question.\",\"limits\":\"The verse does not use الصراط المستقيم or specify that every aspect of guidance is reducible to the stated dispute with the partners.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-11:19\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"These people obstruct God’s way and want it crooked; this directly opposes the focus’s request for a straight path. The passage makes deviation something actively sought and imposed on others.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"},{\"ref\":\"11:19\",\"ar\":\"ٱلَّذِينَ يَصُدُّونَ عَن سَبِيلِ ٱللَّهِ وَيَبْغُونَهَا عِوَجًۭا\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus’s مستقيم and the target’s عوجا provide opposed descriptions of direction, while both name God’s way.\",\"limits\":\"The target describes obstruction by a particular class of people; it does not say that every deviation has the same cause.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-11:56\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The verse predicates that the Lord Himself is عَلَىٰ صِرَاطٍ مُسْتَقِيمٍ. This permits hearing the requested route as aligned with the Lord’s own just order or way, rather than merely as a route external to Him.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"},{\"ref\":\"11:56\",\"ar\":\"إِنَّ رَبِّى عَلَىٰ صِرَٰطٍۢ مُّسْتَقِيمٍۢ\"}],\"activation\":\"The exact صراط مستقيم wording recurs, but 11:56 places the Lord upon it as the subject of the statement.\",\"limits\":\"The excerpt does not settle the precise force of عَلَىٰ or establish a spatial meaning for the Lord’s relation to the path.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-11:112\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The command فَٱسْتَقِمْ uses the same root as ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ. The straight path thus resonates with steadfast upright conduct under command, immediately bounded by the warning not to transgress.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"},{\"ref\":\"11:112\",\"ar\":\"فَٱسْتَقِمْ كَمَآ أُمِرْتَ وَمَن تَابَ مَعَكَ وَلَا تَطْغَوْا۟\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus names the path with an active participle from ق و م; 11:112 commands the Prophet and those with him using the related verb.\",\"limits\":\"Shared root does not make the path noun and the command interchangeable in meaning.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-12:108\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The Prophet names his way as a call to Allah made with insight, shared by those who follow him. This opens a reading of the focus’s plural request as a community asking to be guided along a publicly taught way.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"},{\"ref\":\"12:108\",\"ar\":\"قُلْ هَٰذِهِۦ سَبِيلِىٓ أَدْعُوٓا۟ إِلَى ٱللَّهِ ۚ عَلَىٰ بَصِيرَةٍ أَنَا۠ وَمَنِ ٱتَّبَعَنِى\"}],\"activation\":\"Both verses concern a way and plural participation; 12:108 describes following the Prophet in a call to Allah.\",\"limits\":\"سبيل in 12:108 is not the same noun as صراط in the focus, and the verse does not identify the focus’s speakers with the Prophet’s followers alone.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-13:7\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"The focus says ‘guide us’ in the plural, while 13:7 states that every people has a guide. This places the petition naturally on a communal scale.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"},{\"ref\":\"13:7\",\"ar\":\"وَلِكُلِّ قَوْمٍ هَادٍ\"}],\"activation\":\"The plural object suffix in ٱهْدِنَا and the collective phrase لِكُلِّ قَوْمٍ make the shared scope audible.\",\"limits\":\"13:7 does not identify the guide in each case or say that every people follows the same path.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-14:1\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Revelation is sent to bring people from darkness to light, by their Lord’s permission, toward the path of the Mighty, the Praiseworthy. This joins the focus’s requested direction with scripture-mediated movement and a divinely characterized destination.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"},{\"ref\":\"14:1\",\"ar\":\"لِتُخْرِجَ ٱلنَّاسَ مِنَ ٱلظُّلُمَٰتِ إِلَى ٱلنُّورِ بِإِذْنِ رَبِّهِمْ إِلَىٰ صِرَٰطِ ٱلْعَزِيزِ ٱلْحَمِيدِ\"}],\"activation\":\"Both formulations lead people toward a path; 14:1 explicitly connects that movement to a revealed Book and the Lord’s permission.\",\"limits\":\"The path in 14:1 is qualified by different divine names and is not explicitly called مستقيم.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-14:5\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"Within Surah 14, Moses is commanded to bring his people from darkness to light, echoing 14:1’s account of the Book leading people toward a path. This chain may make the focus’s communal plea resonate with prophetic liberation and guidance.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"},{\"ref\":\"14:1\",\"ar\":\"لِتُخْرِجَ ٱلنَّاسَ مِنَ ٱلظُّلُمَٰتِ إِلَى ٱلنُّورِ\"},{\"ref\":\"14:5\",\"ar\":\"أَنْ أَخْرِجْ قَوْمَكَ مِنَ ٱلظُّلُمَٰتِ إِلَى ٱلنُّورِ\"}],\"missing\":\"The focus mentions neither darkness nor liberation, and 14:5 does not mention a straight path. A passage explicitly joining this exodus imagery to the صراط مستقيم formula could establish the proposed chain.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-14:12\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The messengers recall that Allah has guided them along their ways, then vow patience under harm and place their trust in Him. The focus’s petition is paired here with remembered guidance as the ground for resilience.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"},{\"ref\":\"14:12\",\"ar\":\"وَقَدْ هَدَىٰنَا سُبُلَنَا ۚ وَلَنَصْبِرَنَّ عَلَىٰ مَآ ءَاذَيْتُمُونَا\"}],\"activation\":\"Both passages use the guidance root; 14:12 makes guidance retrospective and joins it to plural ways, patience, and trust.\",\"limits\":\"The messengers’ plural سُبُلَنَا does not define the focus’s singular path or imply that each way is identical.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-14:27\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"Allah makes believers firm by the firm word and leads wrongdoers astray. This may add endurance to the focus’s upright-path image, but the relation is conceptual rather than a shared path expression.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"},{\"ref\":\"14:27\",\"ar\":\"يُثَبِّتُ ٱللَّهُ ٱلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوا۟ بِٱلْقَوْلِ ٱلثَّابِتِ فِى ٱلْحَيَوٰةِ ٱلدُّنْيَا وَفِى ٱلْءَاخِرَةِ ۖ وَيُضِلُّ ٱللَّهُ ٱلظَّٰلِمِينَ\"}],\"missing\":\"The verse does not mention the straight path or use the root ق و م. A passage that joins تثبيت or the firm word to guidance along the straight path could establish a stronger link.\",\"support\":\"weak\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-14:28\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"The next verse describes people who exchange God’s blessing for disbelief and bring their people to the abode of ruin. In sequence after 14:27’s guidance and misguidance, this could serve as an example of a disastrous departure from the focus’s requested way.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"},{\"ref\":\"14:27\",\"ar\":\"وَيُضِلُّ ٱللَّهُ ٱلظَّٰلِمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"14:28\",\"ar\":\"أَلَمْ تَرَ إِلَى ٱلَّذِينَ بَدَّلُوا۟ نِعْمَتَ ٱللَّهِ كُفْرًۭا وَأَحَلُّوا۟ قَوْمَهُمْ دَارَ ٱلْبَوَارِ\"}],\"missing\":\"The passage does not explicitly identify these people with those led astray in 14:27 or describe their history as leaving a straight path. An explicit link between the groups or a path term could decide the reading.\",\"support\":\"weak\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-14:40\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Ibrahim asks to be made one who establishes prayer, using a form from the root ق و م shared by ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ. In a Fatiha recited as prayer, this makes the requested upright way resonate with sustained worship, without equating the path with prayer itself.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:5\",\"ar\":\"إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ وَإِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"},{\"ref\":\"14:40\",\"ar\":\"رَبِّ ٱجْعَلْنِى مُقِيمَ ٱلصَّلَوٰةِ وَمِن ذُرِّيَّتِى\"}],\"activation\":\"The shared root is paired with prayer in Ibrahim’s petition, while 1:5–6 moves from worship and reliance to the request for the straight path.\",\"limits\":\"The root relation and prayer context do not establish that ٱلصِّرَٰطَ means إقامة الصلاة.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-15:24\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-15:41\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"In the exchange with Iblis, the statement ‘this is a path upon Me, straight’ can present the path as held by God’s own guarantee; the following verse denies Iblis authority over God’s servants except those who follow him among the deviators. This makes the plea resonate with divine protection along the route.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"},{\"ref\":\"15:40\",\"ar\":\"إِلَّا عِبَادَكَ مِنْهُمُ ٱلْمُخْلَصِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"15:41\",\"ar\":\"قَالَ هَٰذَا صِرَٰطٌ عَلَىَّ مُسْتَقِيمٌ\"},{\"ref\":\"15:42\",\"ar\":\"إِنَّ عِبَادِى لَيْسَ لَكَ عَلَيْهِمْ سُلْطَٰنٌ إِلَّا مَنِ ٱتَّبَعَكَ مِنَ ٱلْغَاوِينَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The exact path-and-straightness formula occurs within a scene about Iblis’s effort to mislead and the limit of his authority.\",\"limits\":\"The unusual عَلَىَّ does not by itself settle whether the phrase states a divine guarantee or another relation; that syntax needs further support.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-15:87\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"If the ‘seven oft-repeated’ refers to the Fatiha, the focus’s request belongs to a repeatedly recited prayer, so its imperative can be heard as renewed dependence rather than a one-time request.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"},{\"ref\":\"15:87\",\"ar\":\"وَلَقَدْ ءَاتَيْنَٰكَ سَبْعًۭا مِّنَ ٱلْمَثَانِى وَٱلْقُرْءَانَ ٱلْعَظِيمَ\"}],\"missing\":\"15:87 does not name the Fatiha or identify which of its verses is meant. Establishing that the seven refer to the Fatiha would support the repetition link; a further indication that this petition is central to that repetition would sharpen it.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-15:95\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-16:9\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The verse sets قصد السبيل against a way described as جائر, then says Allah could have guided everyone. This supplies a contrast between direct and deviating direction alongside the focus’s plea for the straight route.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"},{\"ref\":\"16:9\",\"ar\":\"وَعَلَى ٱللَّهِ قَصْدُ ٱلسَّبِيلِ وَمِنْهَا جَآئِرٌۭ ۚ وَلَوْ شَآءَ لَهَدَىٰكُمْ أَجْمَعِينَ\"}],\"activation\":\"Both verses concern a path and divine guidance; the target explicitly contrasts one way with a deviating one.\",\"limits\":\"16:9 does not use مستقيم, so the precise relation between قصد and the focus’s description depends on the sense of قصد as directness.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-16:27\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-16:36\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Messengers call every community to worship Allah and avoid false gods; the verse then divides people into those Allah guided and those upon whom error became due. This joins the focus’s plea to a recurring communal summons and divergent responses.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:5\",\"ar\":\"إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ وَإِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"},{\"ref\":\"16:36\",\"ar\":\"وَلَقَدْ بَعَثْنَا فِى كُلِّ أُمَّةٍۢ رَّسُولًا أَنِ ٱعْبُدُوا۟ ٱللَّهَ وَٱجْتَنِبُوا۟ ٱلطَّٰغُوتَ ۖ فَمِنْهُم مَّنْ هَدَى ٱللَّهُ وَمِنْهُم مَّنْ حَقَّتْ عَلَيْهِ ٱلضَّلَٰلَةُ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus moves from worshipping God to asking for guidance; 16:36 joins that same worship call to the outcomes of guidance and error across communities.\",\"limits\":\"The verse’s historical summary does not explain how each community responds or equate all its contexts with the Fatiha’s recitation.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-16:76\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The parable contrasts a man who brings no good wherever he is directed with one who commands justice and is upon a straight path. It distinguishes mere direction from morally effective guidance, giving the requested path a practical mark in justice.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"},{\"ref\":\"16:76\",\"ar\":\"أَيْنَمَا يُوَجِّههُّ لَا يَأْتِ بِخَيْرٍ\"},{\"ref\":\"16:76\",\"ar\":\"وَهُوَ عَلَىٰ صِرَٰطٍۢ مُّسْتَقِيمٍۢ\"}],\"activation\":\"The scene contrasts being directed with being upon the straight path and commanding justice.\",\"limits\":\"The parable does not define every property of the focus’s path through the one quality of justice named here.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-16:121\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Ibrahim is grateful, chosen by God, and guided to a straight path. This makes the focus’s requested guidance visible in an exemplary life that joins divine selection with gratitude.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"},{\"ref\":\"16:121\",\"ar\":\"شَاكِرًۭا لِّأَنْعُمِهِ ۚ ٱجْتَبَىٰهُ وَهَدَىٰهُ إِلَىٰ صِرَٰطٍۢ مُّسْتَقِيمٍۢ\"}],\"activation\":\"The same صراط مستقيم formula is attached to Ibrahim, whose gratitude and selection characterize the surrounding account.\",\"limits\":\"The verse presents Ibrahim’s case and does not say that every guided person has his exact history or role.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-17:9\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The Quran guides toward what is أقوم, an elative from the same root as ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ. The focus’s requested straightness can therefore resonate with the Quran’s own guidance toward what is most upright.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"},{\"ref\":\"17:9\",\"ar\":\"إِنَّ هَٰذَا ٱلْقُرْءَانَ يَهْدِى لِلَّتِى هِىَ أَقْوَمُ\"}],\"activation\":\"The shared root ق و م joins the focus’s path qualifier to the Quran’s description of what it guides toward.\",\"limits\":\"17:9 does not identify the relative phrase لِلَّتِى هِىَ أَقْوَمُ with the specific noun صراط.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-17:35\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The same adjective المستقيم qualifies a balance used to measure and weigh justly. This gives the straightness requested in 1:6 a concrete resonance in fair dealings, without reducing the path to a commercial rule.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"},{\"ref\":\"17:35\",\"ar\":\"وَأَوْفُوا۟ ٱلْكَيْلَ إِذَا كِلْتُمْ وَزِنُوا۟ بِٱلْقِسْطَاسِ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمِ\"}],\"activation\":\"The exact adjective recurs in a command about full measure and just weighing.\",\"limits\":\"The shared qualifier does not establish that the path itself is a measuring instrument or that this verse exhausts rectitude.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-17:97\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The guided person is called المهتدي, while those whom God misguides are gathered blind, mute, and deaf. This makes the opposite of the focus’s request a condition of profound disorientation and loss of access to signs.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"},{\"ref\":\"17:97\",\"ar\":\"وَمَن يَهْدِ ٱللَّهُ فَهُوَ ٱلْمُهْتَدِ ۖ وَمَن يُضْلِلْ فَلَن تَجِدَ لَهُمْ أَوْلِيَآءَ مِن دُونِهِۦ ۖ وَنَحْشُرُهُمْ يَوْمَ ٱلْقِيَٰمَةِ عَلَىٰ وُجُوهِهِمْ عُمْيًۭا وَبُكْمًۭا وَصُمًّۭا\"}],\"activation\":\"The paired hidaya and misguidance clauses connect directly with the focus’s request to be guided.\",\"limits\":\"The scene describes a particular outcome and does not define every form of misguidance as sensory impairment.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}"}}
